course: dictionary
lesson: 8834
title: Shinto, the old religion of Japan
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Shinto is a religion. It is the traditional religion of Japan, and it is a name for a set of beliefs and customs. «Many Indigenous peoples, and the Shinto tradition of Japan, believe that nature has spirits.» The words tradition and Japan are clues: they tell you Shinto is an old belief that belongs to one country.
explain: Shinto followers honour spirits called kami. Kami are believed to live in nature, in mountains, trees and rivers, and in places and in ancestors. So when you read about kami, think of spirits that are part of the natural world around us.
explain: Compare the two sentences. «Christians believe God made nature and cares for it.» The Christian idea is that a god made nature, while the Shinto idea is that spirits live inside nature. The word but is not in the sentence, yet the two ideas are set side by side to show a difference.
fact: Shinto is the traditional religion of Japan.
fact: Shinto followers honour spirits called kami, which are believed to live in nature, in places and in ancestors.
fact: Shinto shrines are marked by a red gate called a torii.
fact: Followers wash their hands and mouth before they pray at a shrine.
fact: Shinto is often studied alongside Buddhism, because many Japanese people follow both.
exercise: «Many Indigenous peoples, and the Shinto tradition of Japan, believe that nature has spirits.» What is Shinto? | The name of a river in Japan | A sport played with a red ball | *The traditional religion of Japan, which honours spirits called kami | A type of Japanese food
exercise: Followers visit a Shinto shrine. What marks a Shinto shrine? | A tall white tower | A wooden bridge over a pond | *A red gate called a torii | A stone wall with a bell
exercise: What do Shinto followers believe about kami? | They should be feared and avoided | They live only in Christian churches | *They should be respected | They are only found in books
test: «Christians believe God made nature and cares for it.» Why is this sentence next to the Shinto idea? | *To show how the two beliefs about nature differ | To show that Christians visit shrines | To show that God lives in Japan | To show they are the same in every way
test: What do Shinto followers do before they pray at a shrine? | Climb the torii | *Wash their hands and mouth | Sing loudly at the gate | Eat a special meal
exam: Which word names the Shinto festivals? | torii | kami | shrine | *matsuri
